  "limitations": [
    "SUBSTITUTE, NOT BDPA. No number here estimates reference-alignment accuracy; the six published percentages are unreachable without the BDPA corpus.",
    "The old basis has fitted Phatak weights (metric16.json) and its coordinates were hand-tuned with Phatak visible; Phatak is only a partly blind test for it.",
    "The new basis has NO fitted weights. z-euclid and z-whiten are assumptions, not fitted quantities; they were chosen and declared before results were read, but a third reasonable metric could reverse the ordering. Gate 1 already shows that: the new basis wins every rank metric and loses the likelihood metric.",
    "Gate 3 fits only a diagonal per-coordinate weighting. It cannot reward what the new basis claims for interacting coordinates, so it under-sells the new basis by construction.",
    "feat_acc is near-saturated because 38 PhonoLib columns are mostly negative; read act_prec, whose per-fold mode baseline floor is reported, not feat_acc.",
    "The ielex fold holds out 86 of the 133 symbols and leaves 8 for the metric fit. It is reported and separately excluded from the excl_thin means rather than being silently averaged in.",
    "No consonant acoustic data exists yet, so nothing here tests whether the tongue coordinates are acoustically live; Gate 3 tests only whether they carry the right phonological geometry.",
    "No significance test is reported. With 9 folds the per-fold spread is the honest uncertainty statement, and it is printed."
  ],
  "runtime_note": {
    "cpu_seconds": 5.69
  },
  "verdict": {
    "question": "Does the new tongue basis beat the old 16-dim basis on identical ground? Answer: not yet, and the pooled Gate-1 hit@1 of 0.39 vs 0.09 that looks like a win is an artefact worth naming.",
    "pooled_gate1_hit1": "new 0.391 vs old 0.094 at unit weights. The cell split shows why: on the 18 Phatak cells whose human confusion was a VOICING pair, the new basis scores hit@1 = 1.000 and the old basis 0.111. The new basis separates voicing perfectly in raw geometry, because voicing moves several tongue coordinates at once.",
    "place_or_manner_cells": "On the 46 remaining cells -- the place and manner confusions, which is what a tongue-shape basis exists to model -- the ordering REVERSES: new 0.152 vs old 0.087 at unit weights, and old 0.239 with its own deployed metric16 weights. The new basis is currently WORSE at place than the old basis is.",
    "likelihood_and_rank_metrics": "Gate 1 NLL and Spearman both favour the old basis (2.40 vs 2.80 nats, rho 0.46 vs 0.29), so the new basis does not win Gate 1 uniformly either.",
    "loco": "Gate 3 LOCO over 9 SVMCC languages, held-out segments removed from the candidate pool: old act_prec 0.649 vs new 0.443 (excluding the degenerate ielex fold). The old basis wins.",
    "what_the_new_basis_does_win": "Voicing separation, and 38 PhonoLib feature geometry only in the soft-pool variant where its own symbols are reachable.",
    "what_this_says_about_the_known_gaps": "Gap 1 (a palatal GROOVE to separate ɕ from ʃ) is confirmed as load-bearing: in the retrieval audit the new basis top-1 is the voicing counterpart for 14 of the 16 Phatak stimuli, and where it is not, it reaches only a coronal sibilant, never a second place. Gap 3 (no consonant acoustics) cannot be tested here at all. This gate adds a fourth: the tongue contour as generated separates voicing well and place poorly, which the generators did not report."
  }
